PG.notes("l068", "<p class=\"summary\">Motivates a design-focused module: users judge a site on looks within seconds, and visual polish can strongly raise perceived value. Previews the four pillars covered next: colour theory, typography, UI design and UX design.</p>\n<h3>Why design matters</h3>\n<ul><li>Functionality and clean code are necessary, but users mostly talk about and remember how a site looks and feels, not how bug-free it is.</li><li>First impressions form within a few seconds, so a visitor judges the brand, product and company almost immediately, comparable to an interview or a first date.</li><li>Case study: a children's juice carton site with clashing colours, mixed typefaces and inconsistent text colours was compared with a redesign by a professional. The redesign used a consistent colour scheme and better font choices.</li><li>In the informal survey she cites, people would pay up to about $1 per carton on the messy site versus about $3 on the redesign, roughly a threefold jump in perceived value from design alone.</li><li>Design is described as one of the cheapest ways to make a product look premium, and getting reasonably good at it does not require a long learning curve.</li></ul>\n<h3>Module roadmap: the four pillars</h3>\n<ul><li>Colour theory: choosing and combining colours so a palette feels consistent.</li><li>Typography: choosing and pairing fonts.</li><li>User interface (UI) design: arranging visual elements to guide attention.</li><li>User experience (UX) design: making the whole journey through the site easy and pleasant.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> These map directly onto CSS skills from earlier sections: colour values, font-family and font properties, spacing and layout, and page structure.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Treating design as an afterthought once the functionality works.</li><li>Using many unrelated colours and typefaces on one page, which makes a site look unprofessional.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l069", "<p class=\"summary\">Colour choice should be deliberate: each dominant colour carries a mood, and colours are combined using relationships on the colour wheel. Covers analogous, complementary, triadic and square schemes, plus tools (Adobe Color, Color Hunt) for getting hex codes.</p>\n<h3>Colour sets the mood</h3>\n<ul><li>The predominant colour of a design communicates a message before any text is read. A palette that contradicts the brand (for example earthy brown on something cheerful) feels off, while a palette matching the message feels intentional.</li><li>Red: love, energy, intensity. Common in advertising for cars and anything meant to feel powerful.</li><li>Yellow: joy, intellect, attention. It is extremely attention-grabbing, so it suits logos and headlines but tires the eyes in long reading areas; avoid a full yellow background behind article text.</li><li>Green: freshness, growth, safety. A safe default for food and grocery brands.</li><li>Blue: stability, trust, serenity. Popular with finance and payments companies; a study she mentions found people wearing blue were trusted more by interviewers.</li><li>Purple: royalty, wealth, femininity. Sometimes used by lenders targeting women.</li><li>Pick colours by asking what message the user should receive, not by personal favourites.</li></ul>\n<h3>Combining colours with the colour wheel</h3>\n<ul><li>Real designs normally use two or three colours, so the relationships between them matter. This is the most systematic part of colour theory.</li><li>Analogous palette: colours adjacent on the wheel. Very harmonious; works for navigation bars, page bodies, and a logo against its background. Weakness: little contrast, so nothing stands out.</li><li>Complementary (clashing) palette: colours from opposite sides of the wheel. Creates strong pop and makes each colour look more vivid (example: red meat displayed against green plastic foliage).</li><li>Do not use complementary colours for text on a background. The vibration is hard to read. Reserve them for logos, icons, accents and elements that should stand apart.</li><li>Triadic palette: three colours forming an equilateral triangle on the wheel. Square palette: four colours forming a square.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Common practical rule: one dominant colour, one secondary, and one accent (roughly 60/30/10 usage).</li></ul>\n<h3>Tools</h3>\n<ul><li>Adobe Color: pick a harmony rule (analogous, complementary, triadic, square) and drag on the wheel until the palette matches the project's mood, then copy the hex codes into your CSS.</li><li>colorhunt.co: curated palettes by designers; browse the popular or trending sections and copy hex codes directly.</li><li>A consistent, brand-appropriate palette makes a site look cohesive and can be playful without feeling chaotic.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Putting a palette into CSS as custom properties</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">:root {\n  --primary: #1f4e9c;   /* blue: trust */\n  --secondary: #5aa9e6; /* analogous lighter blue */\n  --accent: #f2a541;    /* complementary orange for buttons/icons */\n  --text: #1b1b1b;\n  --bg: #ffffff;\n}\nbody { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); }\nbutton { background: var(--accent); color: var(--text); }</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Choosing colours because they are favourites rather than because they suit the message.</li><li>Using complementary colours for text and its background.</li><li>Large yellow backgrounds behind body copy.</li><li>Using too many unrelated hues instead of a small scheme.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l070", "<p class=\"summary\">Font choice carries mood and affects readability. Explains serif versus sans-serif families and their sub-families, what makes type legible, and a simple pairing method: two fonts, similar mood and era, contrasting style or weight.</p>\n<h3>Why typography matters</h3>\n<ul><li>Fonts convey mood just as colours do; the wrong choice sends the wrong message (a cursive script on a serious message is her humorous example).</li><li>Pairing fonts well is one of the cheapest ways to make a design look polished.</li></ul>\n<h3>Serif family</h3>\n<ul><li>Serifs are the small strokes (feet) at the ends of letter strokes. They are traditionally linked to carved stone lettering.</li><li>Mood: traditional, stable, respectable, authoritative, slightly old. Suited to legal letterheads, magazines, architecture and similar.</li><li>Sub-families: Old Style, Transitional, Modern, Slab Serif. A quick way to judge a serif's age is the contrast between the thickest and thinnest parts of a letter: low in Old Style, higher in Transitional, extreme in Modern.</li><li>Modern serifs (Didot is the example, as in a fashion magazine masthead) look authoritative yet contemporary; Old Style suits something aged, such as a winery.</li></ul>\n<h3>Sans-serif family</h3>\n<ul><li>Sans-serif letters end in clean right angles with no feet.</li><li>Mood: friendly, approachable, contemporary, simple, straightforward. Popular with startups.</li><li>Humanist sans-serifs (Gill Sans, Tahoma, Verdana) are known for readability. Designers usually prefer sans-serif for body text.</li><li>A study on car dashboards found that switching from a Grotesque to a Humanist sans-serif cut the time drivers needed to read gauges by roughly 30-40%. The takeaway: legibility has measurable effects, and long copy that is easier to read is more likely to be read to the end.</li></ul>\n<h3>What makes a font legible</h3>\n<ul><li>Open letter shapes with generous spacing between letters.</li><li>Unambiguous forms so similar characters are distinguishable (for example the letter g versus the digit 9).</li><li>Varied proportions, such as a wide capital O versus a narrower zero.</li></ul>\n<h3>Pairing rules</h3>\n<ul><li>Use two fonts per design or per section; ten fonts look cluttered and amateur.</li><li>Pick fonts with a similar mood and from a similar era so they do not feel mismatched.</li><li>Create contrast through serif versus sans-serif (serif heading with sans body, or the reverse).</li><li>Create contrast through weight: a very bold or black heading with light, thin or regular body text.</li><li>She also shows six novelty fonts to avoid in professional work because they are hard to read and their moods are hard to fit; the fonts are shown on a slide and not named in the audio.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A two-font pairing in CSS: serif heading, sans-serif body, contrasting weights</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">h1, h2 {\n  font-family: &quot;Playfair Display&quot;, Georgia, serif;\n  font-weight: 900;\n}\nbody {\n  font-family: &quot;Open Sans&quot;, Verdana, sans-serif;\n  font-weight: 300;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using many fonts in one design.</li><li>Pairing fonts of clashing eras or moods.</li><li>Using decorative novelty fonts for body copy or professional sites.</li><li>Setting long body text in a font with tight spacing or ambiguous characters.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l071", "<p class=\"summary\">UI design directs where users look. Five principles: visual hierarchy, layout, alignment, white space, and designing for the target audience. Each is illustrated with real-site examples and simple rules of thumb.</p>\n<h3>1. Hierarchy</h3>\n<ul><li>Eyes go first to what is bigger, bolder and higher in contrast. Ranking information by importance lets people skim instead of reading everything.</li><li>If all text has equal weight (as in a plain invitation paragraph), the reader must read it all to find the key facts. With hierarchy, the key facts (whose event, where, when) come first.</li><li>Colour: bright, high-contrast colours signal importance; muted or grey tones signal less importance. An ecommerce example keeps a neutral grey/white palette and uses a green accent to pull attention to the add-to-bag action.</li><li>Size: the largest elements get noticed first. A crypto exchange landing page makes its headline biggest, then the email field and Get started button, because capturing the visitor's email is the goal.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In CSS terms, hierarchy comes from font-size, font-weight, colour, contrast and spacing.</li></ul>\n<h3>2. Layout</h3>\n<ul><li>A wall of uniform text is dull. Mix text blocks with images and vary shapes, sizes and emphasis to keep interest.</li><li>Chop content into smaller pieces so visitors absorb more of the page (a grammar tool site is the positive example, an encyclopedia page the negative one).</li><li>Line length: aim for about 40-60 characters per line. Longer lines make the eye lose its track; shorter ones feel choppy.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In CSS, limit line length with max-width in ch units, for example max-width: 60ch.</li></ul>\n<h3>3. Alignment</h3>\n<ul><li>Elements should share clear starting edges. A centred title above left-aligned body text looks mismatched; giving both the same left edge looks deliberate.</li><li>Reduce the number of distinct alignment lines in a design. Draw an imaginary line through the start of each item and merge lines where possible.</li><li>Better alignment alone, with no change to colour or fonts, makes a design look more professional.</li></ul>\n<h3>4. White space</h3>\n<ul><li>White space is the empty area around text and elements. Luxury shops use it to signal quality, while crowded displays suggest a discount store.</li><li>The same applies to digital ads: stuffing a headphone ad with badges and prices looks cheap; spacing it out looks premium and minimalist.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In CSS use padding, margin, gap and line-height to create it.</li></ul>\n<h3>5. Know your audience</h3>\n<ul><li>Style rules depend on who the site is for. A very severe black-and-white children's concert poster fails, while a colourful playful one fits.</li><li>Be flexible: teenagers buying clothes, crypto traders and a medical site each need a different visual style.</li><li>A bolder, messier style is fine when it suits the audience.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> CSS for hierarchy, line length and white space</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">article {\n  max-width: 60ch;      /* ~40-60 characters per line */\n  margin: 0 auto;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n  padding: 4rem 1rem;   /* generous white space */\n}\nh1 { font-size: 3rem; font-weight: 800; }\np  { color: #555; }\n.cta {\n  background: #1db954;  /* single bright accent draws the eye */\n  color: #fff;\n  padding: 0.8rem 2rem;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Giving every element the same visual weight.</li><li>Very long text lines and walls of text.</li><li>Mixing centre and left alignment without reason, or having many different alignment lines.</li><li>Cramming content together with no breathing room.</li><li>Applying one style regardless of audience.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l072", "<p class=\"summary\">UX is the invisible quality of a site that lets people do what they want without effort. Covers five UX principles (simplicity, consistency, reading patterns, all-platform design, testing) and a tour of dark patterns to avoid.</p>\n<h3>UX versus visual design</h3>\n<ul><li>Visual design imposes an aesthetic; good UX is nearly invisible because everything just feels easy.</li><li>A campus-path story illustrates the idea: wait to see where people actually walk, then pave those routes. Design around observed behaviour, not assumptions.</li></ul>\n<h3>1. Simplicity</h3>\n<ul><li>Prefer the simpler option whenever you choose between simple and complex.</li><li>Contrast: a very dense news portal packed with links overwhelms the reader, while a magazine-style news site breaks content into varied chunks (text, images, video, quotes) with a consistent colour scheme and typography.</li></ul>\n<h3>2. Consistency</h3>\n<ul><li>Keep both look and behaviour consistent across the site. Example: a telecom company's pages each had a differently styled navigation bar with moved buttons and a relocating search box, forcing users to relearn the site on each section.</li><li>Inconsistency hurts most for less technical or older users.</li></ul>\n<h3>3. Reading patterns</h3>\n<ul><li>F-pattern: the eye scans across the top, then a shorter scan lower down, then moves down the left edge. Put key content, bullets, icons and buttons along the left, with the logo at top-left. Suited to text-heavy pages.</li><li>Z-pattern: the eye zigzags from top-left to top-right, diagonally down, then across the bottom. Suited to sparser pages with more media or fast browsing; a social network landing page is the example.</li><li>Place content where people naturally look.</li></ul>\n<h3>4. All-platform design</h3>\n<ul><li>Sites must work on phones and desktops: mobile responsive layouts that rearrange rather than just shrinking.</li><li>A desktop layout squeezed onto a phone gives tiny links that are hard to tap. The reverse also fails: a narrow mobile-style layout on a wide monitor leaves vast unused space.</li><li>Avoid stacking so many banners, cookie notices and warnings on mobile that the actual content area is tiny. Equally, do not scale a single image or text block to fill an enormous scroll.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In CSS this is done with the viewport meta tag, media queries, flexible units and max-width containers.</li></ul>\n<h3>5. Test with real people</h3>\n<ul><li>Get feedback from professional testers if possible, otherwise friends and family. Observe what confuses them and iterate.</li></ul>\n<h3>Dark patterns</h3>\n<ul><li>Dark patterns use design psychology to push users toward actions that benefit the company rather than the user. They count as bad UX.</li><li>Misleading hierarchy: highlighting the paid express delivery button in a bright colour so people click it instead of the free option they wanted.</li><li>Disguised ads: a one-pixel line in a mobile ad that looks like a hair on the screen so that swiping at it registers as a click.</li><li>Hidden opt-outs: an airline checkout where the 'no travel insurance' choice is buried in a long alphabetical country-style dropdown rather than shown as an obvious option.</li><li>Confusing double-negative checkbox wording so users cannot tell what ticking the box will do.</li><li>Good design helps people do what they want to do; the packaging analogy is a sandwich that looks full-size but is mostly air.</li></ul>\n<h3>Lead-in to the project</h3>\n<ul><li>The next lecture applies colour, typography, UI and UX to designing a hotel website from scratch.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Basic responsive setup in HTML/CSS</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">/* HTML head must contain: &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1&quot;&gt; */\n.container { max-width: 1100px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 1rem; }\n.cards { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1rem; }\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  nav ul { flex-direction: column; }\n  .cards &gt; * { flex: 1 1 100%; }\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Cramming dense link lists or content onto one page.</li><li>Navigation that changes style or behaviour between sections.</li><li>Designing only for desktop, or only for mobile.</li><li>Stacking intrusive banners on mobile.</li><li>Skipping user testing.</li><li>Using dark patterns such as hidden opt-outs and misleading button emphasis.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l073", "<p class=\"summary\">A screen-recorded demo of designing a three-screen hotel website in Canva (no code), applying colour, typography, UI and UX principles. Ends by asking the learner to design their own, differently themed hotel site and share the link.</p>\n<h3>Where to practise</h3>\n<ul><li>Daily UI gives a free design prompt each day for 100 days (sign-up screens, flight sites, hotel booking and so on). Collect UI gathers other designers' attempts at the same prompts as inspiration.</li><li>Repetition is what builds design skill; study sites you admire and analyse why they work.</li></ul>\n<h3>Canva setup</h3>\n<ul><li>Sign up for a free canva.com account and create a design with custom dimensions of 1400 px by 800 px. Each such canvas page represents one screen of the site; three pages are used (title, features, contact).</li><li>Canva shows snapping alignment guides when dragging elements; use them to line things up to a shared edge or centre.</li></ul>\n<h3>Page 1: title screen</h3>\n<ul><li>Start from a template for a texture/pattern, then replace the plain background with a full-bleed beach photo, resized to touch all edges.</li><li>Add geometric shapes (circle, a white square/frame lined up to the circle's centre) and a gradient to create a modern look and a restrained palette of greens, turquoise, blue and white.</li><li>Hotel name in a serif font (Cardo) in white, very large (around 120-144 pt before trimming), all caps, with increased letter spacing. Serif gives an established, authoritative feel.</li><li>Tagline below in a sans-serif (Glacial Indifference), white, to contrast with the serif title. Both lines left-aligned and snapped to the same left edge.</li><li>Principles used: colour mood, serif/sans contrast, alignment, hierarchy (name biggest).</li></ul>\n<h3>Page 2: features screen</h3>\n<ul><li>Three photos (bedroom, breakfast, pool), cropped and resized to the same size, then spaced evenly using the grid guides: left, middle, right.</li><li>Frame each image with a circle shape behind or around it and a background colour; build one then copy it to the others for consistency.</li><li>Subheading under each image (beautiful rooms, food, pool), left-aligned to the image edge, in the same sans-serif as the title page, letter spacing around 18.</li><li>Short placeholder body text (Lorem Ipsum), trimmed so it is not overwhelming, left-aligned, same font, a muted dark-grey colour so it does not compete with headings. Copy the styled block to all three columns.</li><li>Resulting hierarchy: hotel name, then subheadings, then images, then body text. Short text blocks and pictures keep the layout interesting.</li></ul>\n<h3>Page 3: contact screen</h3>\n<ul><li>A photo (a pineapple) positioned so only the lower part shows, giving a fashionable, tropical feel.</li><li>A heading ('Stay') copied from earlier pages to keep style, palette and typography consistent; shrink it to fit.</li><li>Reuse the geometric shape language: a hexagon in white, resized to contain the contact details.</li><li>Address, email and phone number right-aligned and aligned with the heading. Expect to nudge sizes and spacing; rules are guidelines that need flexibility.</li></ul>\n<h3>Publishing</h3>\n<ul><li>Canva's Publish option, then Website with the Standard style, turns the pages into one scrolling web page with navigation.</li><li>Limitations: no mobile optimisation and no JavaScript or real functionality. It is useful for quickly visualising and sharing a design for feedback.</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Design your own hotel website in Canva</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A free Canva account and the four design pillars from the module. No code or starter files.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Produce a three-screen hotel website design in a different style from the demo, for example a traditional, decadent historic hotel, and share the Canva link.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create a Canva design at 1400 x 800 px.</li><li>Page 1: choose a background photo, a palette that fits the hotel's mood, a serif name with a sans-serif tagline, both aligned to one left edge.</li><li>Page 2: add three equally sized, evenly spaced feature images with consistent frames, subheadings and short muted body text, all aligned.</li><li>Page 3: add a contact screen reusing the same shapes, fonts and colours, with address, email and phone aligned to the heading.</li><li>Check each principle: colour mood, two fonts with contrast, hierarchy, alignment, white space, audience.</li><li>Optionally publish as a Website (Standard) to preview, then share the design link in the course comments.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A cohesive three-screen design with consistent palette, typography and shapes, strong hierarchy and clean alignment, with a visibly different style from the demo.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Mixing fonts or colours between screens instead of reusing the same ones.</li><li>Leaving elements slightly off-grid; use Canva's snapping guides.</li><li>Overlong body text that makes the layout heavy.</li><li>Expecting the Canva site to be responsive or interactive.</li></ul>");
